  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nScattered showers are currently moving north throughout the \nNorthland, though they are still fighting some dry air. \nScattered showers will continue into this evening and tonight as\nTrick or Treaters head out. While there may be a shower here and\nthere, Trick or Treaters will not have to worry about freezing\ntemperatures, as warm air advection will keep temperatures in \nthe upper 40s and lower 50s with lower 40s near Lake Superior. \nOverall rainfall amounts for the rest of the day and overnight \nwill stay light at a tenth of an inch or less, though patchy fog\nwill likely develop across the area late tonight where rain\nfalls. Fog is most likely to form where rain falls throughout\nthe day today.\n\nOn Sunday, any lingering morning drizzle and patchy fog will burn\noff as a warm front lifts north into Ontario. A brief period of\npartial clearing will allow mild air to spread over our region,\nsending afternoon highs into the lower to middle 60s for many \ninland locations. Skies will turn mostly cloudy again late \nSunday night as the next storm system gathers over the Plains.\n\nWidespread rain showers are still expected Monday into Tuesday. A\nstrong plume of moisture from the Gulf and an atmospheric river\nwill bring a soaking rainfall across the area, as precipitable\nwater values are near the climatological maximum for this time\nof year across the Northland. Quick changes in pressure ahead \nof the system will lead to windy conditions with the rain, \nleading to winds of 20-30 mph Monday daytime. Isolated embedded\nstorms are possible Tuesday as instability is added to the mix.\nSevere weather is not anticipated, but also cannot be \ncompletely ruled out at this time. Showers will remain likely \nTuesday night into Wednesday as an upper low dropping out of the\nCanadian Prairies interacts with the system over the Upper \nMidwest. Rain will gradually taper off from west to east through\nWednesday afternoon as the center of low pressure slowly \ndeparts into the Great Lakes, leaving damp and cloudy conditions\nacross the region. Most of the area has at least an 80% chance \nfor an inch or more of rain. However, chances for 2 inches or \nhigher have decreased slightly, and expected totals have \ndecreased slightly as well. These probabilities will be \nmonitored of how they adjust as the event nears.\n\nHigh pressure will build into the Northland for Thursday and Friday,\nbringing an end to the wet pattern. Drier conditions will prevail\nfor the end of the week, though mostly cloudy skies will linger.\nCooler air filtering in behind the departing system will return\ntemperatures to seasonal norms, with highs retreating into the 40s\nand overnight lows dropping into the upper 20s and lower 30s.",
